  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\u27e8x,y\u27e9={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V,$ then $k=$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x,y\u27e9 & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-1\\cdot (-2))+4\\cdot (1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-4+8+4k \\\\  & =4+4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\u27e8x,y\u27e9=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x,y\u27e9 & =0 \\\\ 4+4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =-4 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
